The Renaissance icon packs (neo-launcher/iconpacks/renaissance-*), generated, two scripts:

    python3 -I make-tiles.py           ../../neo-launcher/iconpacks/renaissance-day        --mode day
    python3 -I make-tiles.py           ../../neo-launcher/iconpacks/renaissance-night      --mode night
    python3 -I make-renaissance-pack.py ../../neo-launcher/iconpacks/renaissance-glyph-day   --mode day
    python3 -I make-renaissance-pack.py ../../neo-launcher/iconpacks/renaissance-glyph-night --mode night

Tile (make-tiles.py; the launcher's home grid and dock, `de.yesman.neo icon-pack`): the mock-up's framed icon —
a neon halo outside a bevelled platinum frame with diamond points at the edge centres, a marble plate with gold acanthus
corners and gear clusters, a marble/platinum disc with sacred-geometry wires, and the app's fine-line Material glyph
tinted toward its neon — 256 px squares. Day is `milk` (a milky, slightly see-through plate, ink glyph), night `smoke`
(a dark smoked plate, pale-gold glyph and full neon); Minerva's own icon (`yesman`) is the owl (owl.png, derived from the
Minerva agent image) in the agent's toothed gear ring. Every app in gnome-map.json has a glyph and a neon in the
generator's TILED table; the frame and neon are part of the icon, so pack.json has no `iconback` and the scale is moot
(the launcher, neo-launcher/src/icons.c icons_render, paints a drawn icon at the full icon size over nothing).
icons/iconback.png is still written for the file set, but pack.json does not list it.

Glyph (make-renaissance-pack.py; for Neo Settings' rows and search results, not the launcher): only the inner part —
the fine-line glyph and its neon aura on transparent, in a marble (day) or granite (night) ink. icons/<name>.png for
every icon-name string Neo Settings uses (apps/settings/src, "-symbolic" suffix kept) and the short names of the
mock-up (wifi bt display sound notif battery account lock minerva apps); icons.txt lists them. The colour is the
area's.

Glyphs are Material Symbols Outlined, weight 200 (Google, Apache-2.0: glyphs/LICENSE), fetched by fetch-glyphs.sh from
github.com/google/material-design-icons (symbols/web/<name>/materialsymbolsoutlined/<name>_wght200_48px.svg) and
committed. Both scripts need python3-gi with Rsvg 2.0, python3-cairo and python3-pil (make-tiles.py also python3-numpy).
Output is deterministic (textures are seeded from the icon's name). To add an app: a line in the tile generator's TILED
(glyph, neon) and its desktop id in its MAP; to add a Settings icon: a line in GLYPHS; then run fetch-glyphs.sh and the
four commands above.
